# Cloudflare Pages / Netlify static-headers convention (ignored by other
# hosts — notably GitHub Pages, which serves no custom headers at all; see
# docs/config.md "SVG asset trust model" for what that means for that
# deployment target). Hashed build output is immutable; the WASM is
# version-pinned but served under a stable filename, so it gets a day of
# browser cache (the app's own versioned Cache Storage makes browser-cache
# lifetime mostly moot anyway).
#
# This is only PART of what ships as dist/_headers. vite.config.ts's
# securityHeaders plugin appends a further `/*` block at build time — the app
# document's Content-Security-Policy, X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options,
# Referrer-Policy and Permissions-Policy — computed fresh on every build
# because its script-src needs the built index.html's inline theme-script
# hash, which varies per config (see src/lib/securityHeaders.mjs for the
# policy and its rationale). That block is deliberately not committed here.
# Cloudflare Pages combines every matching rule for a request; where two
# matching rules set the same header name (e.g. this file's /scad/* CSP and
# the appended /* CSP, for a request under /scad/), it joins the values with
# a comma rather than letting one replace the other — for Content-Security-
# Policy specifically, a comma-separated value is a list of policies the
# browser enforces as their INTERSECTION, so both stay in force together. So
# don't be surprised that a freshly built dist/_headers has more in it than
# this file: that's the /* block above, not a change to what's committed here.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
/wasm/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# M13 — design/config sources (the SVGs mounted under /scad/: use/include
# assets consumed by OpenSCAD's import()/surface()) and generated artwork
# (design icon/image, bundled-preset thumbnails, the header logo, under
# /art/; the app icon at /icon.svg) are trusted OPERATOR input, not sandboxed
# third-party content — see docs/config.md "SVG asset trust model". They're
# safe wherever they're actually used (<img>, <use>, CSS, OpenSCAD's WASM
# FS), but an SVG can carry <script>, and a browser that is navigated to one
# *directly* would otherwise execute it in this app's origin. These headers
# are cheap defense-in-depth against exactly that direct-navigation case;
# they don't restrict — and can't break — the app's own <img>/<use>/CSS/
# import() consumption of the same files, since a CSP header only governs the
# fetched resource when *it* is the top-level document (or an embedded
# document, e.g. an iframe), not when it's decoded as an image or mounted
# into the WASM filesystem. (The trailing /*.svg rule below already covers
# every SVG regardless of directory; /scad/* and /art/* additionally cover
# non-SVG files under each, e.g. a bundled-preset PNG, for the same
# defense-in-depth reason.)
/scad/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; sandbox
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
/art/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; sandbox
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
/icon.svg
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; sandbox
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
/*.svg
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; sandbox
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
